  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1116 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft is split between two main features. The \neast-central region of the CONUS is dominated by high pressure and \nridging. Conversely over the Pacific Northwest, there is a \ndeveloping trough. Nearer to the surface, a compact lee side trough \nhas developed over eastern Colorado. Previous model runs in the \nlast couple days hinted at some precipitation potential with this \nlee trough, but the latest runs has it being nearly completely dry. \nOutside for the <5% chance that lingering light rain from Colorado \nsurvives late into the night, no precipitation is forecast tonight.\n\nThe strong south winds will ease somewhat on Wednesday, with winds \nup to 25 mph and gusts up to 35 mph. With the strong WAA brought \nabout by the strong south winds, highs Wednesday will race back up \nto the mid-60s. If winds continue to decrease enough through to \nnight to below 10 mph, fog development is possible. Relative \nhumidity values are expected to be around 100% around 6 AM Thursday \nmorning. Ensembles keep visibilities mostly clear, but the HRRR \nspecifically is very bullish on the fog with visibilities across \nmuch of the CWA down to 1/4 mile. It will be the focus of the near \nterm forecast with little active weather through the period.\n\nThursday and Friday are forecast to continue the highs around the \nmid-60s. This comes with more strong south winds at around 15-25 mph \nand higher gusts. On Saturday, a strong front is forecast to sweep \nacross SW Kansas from the system currently residing over the Pacific \nNorthwest. A lot of uncertainty remains on the precipitation \npotential with the system still being a few days out. Early \nindications from long-term models is that the precipitation will \nstay confined to the eastern zones nearer to central Kansas. This \ncould still change dramatically if the progged ejection of the wave \nis transposed temporally. Regardless of the precipitation, \ntemperatures are forecast to drop significantly with highs of 50s \nSaturday and 40s through the rest of the forecast period.",
